# Written for whoever works on the package, not for whoever installs it.
#
# docs/ is where the working papers live — the handoff, the backlog and the
# records of past waves. doc/ is published: pub.dev
# renders only README, CHANGELOG and LICENSE, but what rides in the archive is
# there for whoever opens the package, and a link from the readme reaches it.
# A .pubignore replaces .gitignore rather than adding to it, so what `dart doc`
# generates has to be named here as well or a local run of it rides along.
#
# tool/ is where the generator of the color tables lives: it needs the
# repository to rewrite lib/, and the package needs only what it wrote.
# benchmark/compare.dart is of the same kind: it checks out two versions of
# the package to weigh them against each other, so it needs the repository
# too, while the benchmark it runs on each side stands on its own.
#
# benchmark/memory_guard.dart is left out for a nearer reason: it fails when a
# reading falls outside a band calibrated on the machine that wrote it down,
# so away from this repository's CI it would be an opinion rather than a
# measurement. The benchmark beside it is the one that says something
# anywhere.
#
# AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md are the same kind of paper as docs/: they tell an
# agent how work is conducted here, which is nothing to whoever installs the
# package. CLAUDE.md only imports AGENTS.md, so the two travel together.
#
# coverage/ is what `dart test --coverage` writes — the CI collects it there
# and a local run puts it in the same place. It is a report about the tests,
# not a part of the package, and the replacement rule above is why naming it
# in .gitignore is not enough.
docs/
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
doc/api/
benchmark/compare.dart
benchmark/memory_guard.dart
tool/
coverage/
